// Dhruva round 74 -- first real vani-compiled code on the Pi 4/5
// (AArch64) port. Deliberately NOT a port of kernel_main.vani (28000+
// lines, written entirely against ARM32/BCM2835 addresses and this
// project's Pi 1 hand-written asm ABI) -- that is genuinely its own
// multi-round effort, exactly as docs/TODO.md's own "Remaining scope
// for a real Pi 4 boot" list already says. This is round 43's own
// "smallest possible proof" philosophy applied to the OTHER half of
// this port's remaining gap: prove the vani-toolchain-to-AArch64
// pipeline itself is genuinely correct end to end, nothing more.
//
// Confirmed this round, live, before writing this file: vani's own
// LLVM backend emits ordinary, target-generic LLVM IR (no ARM32-
// specific assumptions baked in) -- `vanic emit --backend=llvm` on a
// trivial two-function program, then `llc -mtriple=aarch64-none-elf`,
// produced correct, real AArch64 machine code on the first attempt,
// using the exact same "vanic emits IR, llc lowers it" pipeline
// build.sh already established for the Pi 1/ARMv6 target. No changes
// to vani-compiler itself were needed.
//
// Every builtin this file uses (`mmio_read_u32`/`mmio_write_u32`/
// `mmio_write_u8`) is a genuine, target-independent vani-compiler
// intrinsic (raw volatile load/store on a pointer -- see
// ssa_backend_llvm.rs's own handling of these three names), the exact
// same ones kernel_main.vani's own uart_putc/uart_puts already use for
// the Pi 1 target. This file needs ZERO `extern "C" fn` declarations
// and zero hand-written-asm helpers of its own -- the only assembly-
// side dependency at all is `#[no_mangle]` on kmain_rpi4_vani below,
// so boot/rpi4/boot.S's own `bl kmain_rpi4_vani` can find it by its
// bare, unmangled name, exactly the same convention this project's
// Pi 1 side already uses for irq_dispatch/kernel_main.
//
// PL011 UART base is 0xFE201000 here, NOT kernel_main.vani's
// 0x20201000 -- BCM2711's own low-peripheral-mode address (confirmed
// live in round 40's own spike, already load-bearing in boot/rpi4/
// boot.S's uart_puts_rpi4), a different SoC generation from the Pi 1's
// BCM2835. Same PL011 register protocol otherwise (UARTDR at +0x00,
// UARTFR at +0x18, TXFF at bit 5).
//
// DHDL v0.1 MVP (see ~/dhruvaos-device-tree.md / kernel_main.vani's
// own BCM2835 register-map block for the same treatment on the Pi 1
// side): `#[mmio(size=N)]` lets `vanic check` verify these don't
// overlap each other -- only 2 registers on this file's own BCM2711
// slice today, but a real check either way, not just a rename.
// `_RPI4` suffix (matching this file's own `_rpi4_vani` fn-naming
// convention) distinguishes these from kernel_main.vani's same-named
// BCM2835 constants -- different physical addresses, different SoC,
// a genuinely separate compilation unit (build_rpi4.sh never combines
// the two files), so there's no name collision to worry about, only
// a readability one this naming avoids.
#[mmio(size=4)]
const UART_DR_RPI4: i64 = 0xFE201000;
#[mmio(size=4)]
const UART_FR_RPI4: i64 = 0xFE201018;
// Round 77: UART0 RX interrupt (see boot/rpi4/vectors.S's own
// IRQ_UART0_INTID comment for the GIC-side derivation). PL011
// register offsets are the standard ARM PrimeCell layout, already
// load-bearing for TX above -- IMSC at +0x38, ICR at +0x44, same as
// kernel_main.vani's own BCM2835 UART0 (different base, same
// peripheral IP block).
#[mmio(size=4)]
const UART_IMSC_RPI4: i64 = 0xFE201038;
#[mmio(size=4)]
const UART_ICR_RPI4: i64 = 0xFE201044;
// GIC distributor registers needed to route INTID 153 (UART0 RX) to
// CPU0 -- see rpi4_uart_rx_irq_init's own comment for why an SPI
// (unlike the timer's PPI, already enabled in boot/rpi4/gic_timer.S)
// needs both of these, and in this order. GICD_BASE is 0xFF841000
// (same source-derivation as boot/rpi4/gic_timer.S's own GICD_BASE/
// GICC_BASE constants -- see that file's header comment).
// GICD_ISENABLER4 = GICD_BASE + 0x100 + 4*4 (INTID 153 falls in the
// 4th 32-IRQ group: 153/32=4, bit 153%32=25).
#[mmio(size=4)]
const GICD_ISENABLER4_RPI4: i64 = 0xFF841110;
// GICD_ITARGETSR is one byte per IRQ starting at GICD_BASE+0x800;
// this word covers IRQs 152-155, so only byte 1 (IRQ 153) is
// nonzero: 0x00000100 = CPU0's target bit (0x01) in that byte.
#[mmio(size=4)]
const GICD_ITARGETSR_UART0_RPI4: i64 = 0xFF841898;

// Round 76 -- GPIO, the next increment of "port kernel_main.vani (or
// its boot-facing pieces) to Pi 4/5" (docs/TODO.md, marked
// `[PARTIAL, round 75]` until now). Confirmed against QEMU 10.0.0's
// own source before writing anything (same discipline as
// kernel_main.vani's own round-71 GPIO port): `hw/arm/bcm2838_
// peripherals.c` instantiates a genuine `TYPE_BCM2838_GPIO` device
// (`hw/gpio/bcm2838_gpio.c`) at `GPIO_OFFSET` (`0x200000`, from
// `include/hw/arm/raspi_platform.h`) off this SoC's own low-
// peripheral-mode base (`bc_base->peri_base = 0xfe000000`, set in
// `hw/arm/bcm2838.c`) -- base address 0xFE200000. Same GPFSELn/
// GPSET/GPCLR/GPLEV register OFFSETS as BCM2835 (confirmed identical
// in `bcm2838_gpio.c`: GPFSEL1=+0x04, GPSET0=+0x1C, GPCLR0=+0x28,
// GPLEV0=+0x34), so `kernel_main.vani`'s own register-address/bit-
// mask arithmetic for these three families ports over unchanged in
// shape, just against this file's own BCM2711 base + `_RPI4`-suffixed
// consts.
//
// One genuine SoC-generation difference, confirmed from the same
// source: BCM2711 replaced BCM2835's old GPPUD/GPPUDCLK0/1 pull
// dance with 4 new `GPIO_PUP_PDN_CNTRL_REG0..3` registers (2 bits per
// pin, 16 pins/register -- `hw/gpio/bcm2838_gpio.c`'s own
// `GPIO_PUP_PDN_CNTRL_NUM=4` confirms the pin-per-register count).
// Unlike kernel_main.vani's own pull-control code (real, spec-correct,
// but UNVERIFIABLE under QEMU -- BCM2835's GPPUD/GPPUDCLK are
// explicitly "Not implemented" there), THIS register bank is a
// genuine read/write store in QEMU's BCM2838 model (confirmed: real
// non-zero boot-time reset values `RESET_VAL_CNTRL_REG0..3`, not a
// stub), so a round-trip self-test here is real, QEMU-verified
// evidence, not just spec-correct-but-unverifiable code -- a genuine
// advantage of this SoC generation's own design over Pi 1's, not
// assumed symmetric between the two targets.
#[mmio(size=4)]
const GPFSEL0_RPI4: i64 = 0xFE200000;
#[mmio(size=4)]
const GPFSEL1_RPI4: i64 = 0xFE200004;
#[mmio(size=4)]
const GPFSEL5_RPI4: i64 = 0xFE200014;
#[mmio(size=4)]
const GPSET0_RPI4: i64 = 0xFE20001C;
#[mmio(size=4)]
const GPSET1_RPI4: i64 = 0xFE200020;
#[mmio(size=4)]
const GPCLR0_RPI4: i64 = 0xFE200028;
#[mmio(size=4)]
const GPCLR1_RPI4: i64 = 0xFE20002C;
#[mmio(size=4)]
const GPLEV0_RPI4: i64 = 0xFE200034;
#[mmio(size=4)]
const GPLEV1_RPI4: i64 = 0xFE200038;
#[mmio(size=4)]
const GPIO_PUP_PDN_CNTRL_REG0_RPI4: i64 = 0xFE2000E4;
#[mmio(size=4)]
const GPIO_PUP_PDN_CNTRL_REG1_RPI4: i64 = 0xFE2000E8;
#[mmio(size=4)]
const GPIO_PUP_PDN_CNTRL_REG2_RPI4: i64 = 0xFE2000EC;
#[mmio(size=4)]
const GPIO_PUP_PDN_CNTRL_REG3_RPI4: i64 = 0xFE2000F0;

fn uart_putc_rpi4_vani(c: i64) -> i64 {
  let uart_fr: i64 = UART_FR_RPI4;
  let uart_dr: i64 = UART_DR_RPI4;
  while true {
    let fr: u32 = mmio_read_u32(uart_fr);
    if (fr & (0x20 as u32)) == (0 as u32) {
      break;
    }
  }
  let _ = mmio_write_u8(uart_dr, c as u8);
  return 0;
}

fn uart_puts_rpi4_vani(s: Str) -> i64 {
  let n: i64 = str_len_bytes(s);
  let i: i64 = 0;
  while i < n {
    let b: i64 = str_byte_at(s, i);
    let _ = uart_putc_rpi4_vani(b);
    i = i + 1;
  }
  return 0;
}

// Iterative high-order-digit-first printer -- deliberately avoids
// needing any scratch buffer (and therefore any extern "C" allocator
// like kernel_main.vani's own dhruva_alloc_bytes), keeping this
// file's only non-builtin dependency at exactly zero, per this file's
// own header comment.
//
// FOUND LIVE, this round: an earlier version of this function used
// recursion under a `#[bounded(20)]` annotation instead. That
// compiled and type-checked cleanly, but faulted the very first time
// it actually ran (a real Data Abort, permission fault, at FAR_EL1
// near address 0x10) -- root-caused via the emitted LLVM IR
// (`vanic emit --backend=llvm`): `#[bounded(N)]`'s recursion-depth
// counter is an LLVM `thread_local global i32`, and this freestanding
// target has never initialized TPIDR_EL0 (no thread pointer at all --
// nothing in boot/rpi4/boot.S sets one up, matching every other
// hand-written bring-up file in this project, which have never needed
// one). `llc`'s local-exec TLS lowering resolves the access relative
// to that unset (effectively zero) thread pointer, landing near
// address 0 -- exactly matching the observed fault address. This is
// the first place in the ENTIRE DhruvaOS codebase `#[bounded(N)]`
// (recursion-depth, distinct from kernel_main.vani's many
// `#[bounded_stack(bytes=N)]` stack-size budgets) was ever actually
// exercised at runtime -- a genuinely latent gap in vani's bare-metal/
// no-TLS-runtime target support, not something either existing target
// happened to already work around. Not fixed in vani-compiler itself
// this round (would need either compiler support for a non-TLS
// depth-counter mode on freestanding targets, or this project
// initializing a real TPIDR_EL0-backed TLS block -- out of scope for
// this round's own goal of proving the toolchain, not extending it);
// routed around here by simply not using recursion, and noted in
// docs/TODO.md / project memory for any future vani-on-bare-metal
// work that reaches for `#[bounded(N)]`.
fn uart_put_u64_rpi4_vani_digits(v: i64) -> i64 {
  let power: i64 = 1;
  while (power * 10) <= v {
    power = power * 10;
  }
  let remaining: i64 = v;
  while power >= 1 {
    let d: i64 = remaining / power;
    let _ = uart_putc_rpi4_vani(48 + d);
    remaining = remaining - (d * power);
    power = power / 10;
  }
  return 0;
}

fn uart_put_i64_rpi4_vani(v: i64) -> i64 {
  if v == 0 {
    return uart_putc_rpi4_vani(48);
  }
  if v < 0 {
    let _ = uart_putc_rpi4_vani(45); // '-'
    return uart_put_u64_rpi4_vani_digits(0 - v);
  }
  return uart_put_u64_rpi4_vani_digits(v);
}

// Real computation, not a placeholder: sum(1..100)==5050 (Gauss's own
// check on a genuine loop, exactly the kind of thing a from-scratch
// port most needs to prove -- that loops/comparisons/arithmetic all
// compile and execute correctly on real AArch64 codegen, not just
// that a function can be called at all), plus one integer division
// (AArch64 has a hardware SDIV, unlike ARMv6 -- this needs no libgcc
// helper the way kernel_main.vani's own Phase 4 task #10 division
// needed __aeabi_ldivmod on the Pi 1 side).
fn rpi4_vani_self_test() -> i64 {
  let ok: i64 = 1;

  let total: i64 = 0;
  let i: i64 = 1;
  while i <= 100 {
    total = total + i;
    i = i + 1;
  }
  if total != 5050 {
    ok = 0;
  }

  let quotient: i64 = 100000 / 7;
  let remainder: i64 = 100000 - (quotient * 7);
  if (quotient != 14285) || (remainder != 5) {
    ok = 0;
  }

  let _ = uart_puts_rpi4_vani("DHRUVA RPI4: vani-compiled code self-test (sum 1..100 + division)");
  if ok == 1 {
    let _ = uart_puts_rpi4_vani(" (PASS)\n");
  } else {
    let _ = uart_puts_rpi4_vani(" (FAIL)\n");
  }
  let _ = uart_puts_rpi4_vani("DHRUVA RPI4: sum=");
  let _ = uart_put_i64_rpi4_vani(total);
  let _ = uart_puts_rpi4_vani(" quotient=");
  let _ = uart_put_i64_rpi4_vani(quotient);
  let _ = uart_puts_rpi4_vani(" remainder=");
  let _ = uart_put_i64_rpi4_vani(remainder);
  let _ = uart_puts_rpi4_vani("\n");
  return 0;
}

// GPIO function-select: 3 bits/pin, 10 pins/register -- identical
// encoding and packing to BCM2835 (see this file's own header comment
// on the const block above), just against the 3 registers this file
// declares (pins 0-9, 10-19, 50-53). A 3-way branch instead of
// kernel_main.vani's one-line `base + (pin/10)*4` arithmetic on a raw
// literal, since every address here goes through a named, checked
// `#[mmio]` const instead -- consistent with this file's own const
// block, not a style regression.
fn gpio_fsel_reg_for_rpi4(pin: i64) -> i64 {
  if pin < 10 {
    return GPFSEL0_RPI4;
  }
  if pin < 20 {
    return GPFSEL1_RPI4;
  }
  return GPFSEL5_RPI4;
}

fn gpio_fsel_shift_for_rpi4(pin: i64) -> i64 {
  return (pin % 10) * 3;
}

fn gpio_set_function_rpi4(pin: i64, func: u32) -> i64 {
  let reg: i64 = gpio_fsel_reg_for_rpi4(pin);
  let shift: u32 = gpio_fsel_shift_for_rpi4(pin) as u32;
  let mask: u32 = ((7 as u32) << shift) ^ (0xFFFFFFFF as u32);
  let bits: u32 = (func & (7 as u32)) << shift;
  let cur: u32 = mmio_read_u32(reg);
  let _ = mmio_write_u32(reg, (cur & mask) | bits);
  return 0;
}

fn gpio_get_function_rpi4(pin: i64) -> u32 {
  let reg: i64 = gpio_fsel_reg_for_rpi4(pin);
  let shift: u32 = gpio_fsel_shift_for_rpi4(pin) as u32;
  let cur: u32 = mmio_read_u32(reg);
  return (cur >> shift) & (7 as u32);
}

// GPSET/GPCLR are one-shot "set exactly these bits" registers, never
// read-modify-write, matching the hardware's own design -- same shape
// as kernel_main.vani's own gpio_write.
fn gpio_write_rpi4(pin: i64, value: u32) -> i64 {
  let bit: u32 = (pin % 32) as u32;
  let set_reg: i64 = if pin < 32 { GPSET0_RPI4 } else { GPSET1_RPI4 };
  let clr_reg: i64 = if pin < 32 { GPCLR0_RPI4 } else { GPCLR1_RPI4 };
  let reg: i64 = if value != (0 as u32) { set_reg } else { clr_reg };
  let _ = mmio_write_u32(reg, (1 as u32) << bit);
  return 0;
}

fn gpio_read_rpi4(pin: i64) -> u32 {
  let reg: i64 = if pin < 32 { GPLEV0_RPI4 } else { GPLEV1_RPI4 };
  let bit: u32 = (pin % 32) as u32;
  let val: u32 = mmio_read_u32(reg);
  return (val >> bit) & (1 as u32);
}

// Pull-up/down/none control -- BCM2711's own scheme, genuinely
// different from BCM2835's GPPUD/GPPUDCLK dance (see this file's own
// header comment): 2 bits/pin, 16 pins/register, 4 registers. Encoding
// per Broadcom's own BCM2711 documentation: 0b00=none, 0b01=up,
// 0b10=down.
fn gpio_pupdn_reg_for_rpi4(pin: i64) -> i64 {
  let idx: i64 = pin / 16;
  if idx == 0 {
    return GPIO_PUP_PDN_CNTRL_REG0_RPI4;
  }
  if idx == 1 {
    return GPIO_PUP_PDN_CNTRL_REG1_RPI4;
  }
  if idx == 2 {
    return GPIO_PUP_PDN_CNTRL_REG2_RPI4;
  }
  return GPIO_PUP_PDN_CNTRL_REG3_RPI4;
}

fn gpio_pupdn_shift_for_rpi4(pin: i64) -> i64 {
  return (pin % 16) * 2;
}

fn gpio_set_pull_rpi4(pin: i64, pull: u32) -> i64 {
  let reg: i64 = gpio_pupdn_reg_for_rpi4(pin);
  let shift: u32 = gpio_pupdn_shift_for_rpi4(pin) as u32;
  let mask: u32 = ((3 as u32) << shift) ^ (0xFFFFFFFF as u32);
  let bits: u32 = (pull & (3 as u32)) << shift;
  let cur: u32 = mmio_read_u32(reg);
  let _ = mmio_write_u32(reg, (cur & mask) | bits);
  return 0;
}

fn gpio_get_pull_rpi4(pin: i64) -> u32 {
  let reg: i64 = gpio_pupdn_reg_for_rpi4(pin);
  let shift: u32 = gpio_pupdn_shift_for_rpi4(pin) as u32;
  let cur: u32 = mmio_read_u32(reg);
  return (cur >> shift) & (3 as u32);
}

// Self-test, run once at boot -- same shape as kernel_main.vani's own
// gpio_self_check: round-trips saved/restored so this leaves no
// lasting side effect on boot state.
fn gpio_self_check_rpi4() -> i64 {
  let ok: i64 = 1;

  // Pins 0, 10, and 53 span the 3 GPFSELn registers this file
  // declares.
  let orig0: u32 = gpio_get_function_rpi4(0);
  let _ = gpio_set_function_rpi4(0, 1 as u32);
  if gpio_get_function_rpi4(0) != (1 as u32) {
    ok = 0;
  }
  let _ = gpio_set_function_rpi4(0, orig0);

  let orig10: u32 = gpio_get_function_rpi4(10);
  let _ = gpio_set_function_rpi4(10, 1 as u32);
  if gpio_get_function_rpi4(10) != (1 as u32) {
    ok = 0;
  }
  let _ = gpio_set_function_rpi4(10, orig10);

  let orig53: u32 = gpio_get_function_rpi4(53);
  let _ = gpio_set_function_rpi4(53, 1 as u32);
  if gpio_get_function_rpi4(53) != (1 as u32) {
    ok = 0;
  }
  let _ = gpio_set_function_rpi4(53, orig53);

  // Setting one pin's function must not disturb its neighbor packed
  // into the same register -- pins 5 and 6 share GPFSEL0.
  let orig5: u32 = gpio_get_function_rpi4(5);
  let orig6: u32 = gpio_get_function_rpi4(6);
  let _ = gpio_set_function_rpi4(5, 1 as u32);
  let _ = gpio_set_function_rpi4(6, 0 as u32);
  if gpio_get_function_rpi4(5) != (1 as u32) {
    ok = 0;
  }
  let _ = gpio_set_function_rpi4(6, 1 as u32);
  if gpio_get_function_rpi4(5) != (1 as u32) {
    ok = 0;
  }
  let _ = gpio_set_function_rpi4(5, orig5);
  let _ = gpio_set_function_rpi4(6, orig6);

  // GPSET/GPCLR/GPLEV round trip across the GPSET0/GPSET1 (pin 31/32)
  // boundary.
  let _ = gpio_write_rpi4(31, 1 as u32);
  if gpio_read_rpi4(31) != (1 as u32) {
    ok = 0;
  }
  let _ = gpio_write_rpi4(31, 0 as u32);
  if gpio_read_rpi4(31) != (0 as u32) {
    ok = 0;
  }
  let _ = gpio_write_rpi4(32, 1 as u32);
  if gpio_read_rpi4(32) != (1 as u32) {
    ok = 0;
  }
  let _ = gpio_write_rpi4(32, 0 as u32);
  if gpio_read_rpi4(32) != (0 as u32) {
    ok = 0;
  }

  // Pull-control round trip across the REG0/REG1/REG3 (pin 15/16/48)
  // boundaries -- genuinely QEMU-verified here, unlike kernel_main.
  // vani's own BCM2835 equivalent.
  let origp15: u32 = gpio_get_pull_rpi4(15);
  let _ = gpio_set_pull_rpi4(15, 1 as u32);
  if gpio_get_pull_rpi4(15) != (1 as u32) {
    ok = 0;
  }
  let _ = gpio_set_pull_rpi4(15, 2 as u32);
  if gpio_get_pull_rpi4(15) != (2 as u32) {
    ok = 0;
  }
  let _ = gpio_set_pull_rpi4(15, origp15);

  let origp48: u32 = gpio_get_pull_rpi4(48);
  let _ = gpio_set_pull_rpi4(48, 1 as u32);
  if gpio_get_pull_rpi4(48) != (1 as u32) {
    ok = 0;
  }
  let _ = gpio_set_pull_rpi4(48, origp48);

  let _ = uart_puts_rpi4_vani("DHRUVA RPI4: GPIO function-select + GPSET/GPCLR/GPLEV + pull-control round trip (BCM2711 registers, QEMU-modeled)");
  if ok == 1 {
    let _ = uart_puts_rpi4_vani(" (PASS)\n");
  } else {
    let _ = uart_puts_rpi4_vani(" (FAIL)\n");
  }
  return 0;
}

// Round 77 -- UART0 RX, the first bidirectional I/O on this port
// (everything before this was output-only). Three one-time setup
// steps, order matters for the last two:
//
// 1. Unmask the PL011's own RX interrupt (RXIM, bit 4 of IMSC).
//    Deliberately does NOT write UART_CR (unlike kernel_main.vani's
//    own uart_init) -- confirmed against QEMU 10.0.0's own hw/char/
//    pl011.c that this model's pl011_can_receive "effectively
//    keep[s] the UART continuously enabled regardless of the enable
//    bits" (its own comment, verbatim), so TX already works
//    unconditionally on this port (uart_putc_rpi4_vani never writes
//    CR either) and RX will too.
//
// 2. Set GICD_ITARGETSR so INTID 153 routes to CPU0. UNLIKE the
//    timer's PPI (INTID 30, enabled in boot/rpi4/gic_timer.S's own
//    gic_timer_init with no targeting needed -- PPIs are inherently
//    per-CPU by GIC design), an SPI has no implicit target at all.
//
// 3. THEN set GICD_ISENABLER4 bit 25 (153 mod 32) to actually enable
//    it. This order is load-bearing, not stylistic: confirmed by
//    reading QEMU 10.0.0's own hw/intc/arm_gic.c dist_writeb handler
//    for the Interrupt Set-Enable register -- for an SPI, it computes
//    the per-CPU enable mask from GIC_DIST_TARGET(irq) AT THE MOMENT
//    ISENABLER is written, not dynamically later. Enabling before
//    targeting captures a target of zero CPUs, and the interrupt
//    silently never becomes deliverable to any CPU even though
//    GICD_ISENABLER itself correctly reads back as "enabled" --
//    found exactly this way: a temporary polling probe (bypassing
//    the interrupt path entirely) proved real bytes sent over stdin
//    genuinely reached the PL011's RX FIFO while the interrupt never
//    fired, which is what led to reading arm_gic.c's own source
//    rather than guessing further.
fn rpi4_uart_rx_irq_init() -> i64 {
  let _ = mmio_write_u32(UART_IMSC_RPI4, 0x10 as u32);
  let _ = mmio_write_u32(GICD_ITARGETSR_UART0_RPI4, 0x00000100 as u32);
  let _ = mmio_write_u32(GICD_ISENABLER4_RPI4, (1 as u32) << (25 as u32));
  return 0;
}

// Drains the RX FIFO (bounded loop, matching kernel_main.vani's own
// irq_dispatch UART branch shape), echoes each byte straight back out
// (unconditionally -- no su/passwd suppression at this minimal
// scope), and pushes it into the shell's own line buffer
// (boot/rpi4/shell_state.S's shell_rx_push_char -- round 84). Clears
// the PL011's own RXIC status once after draining (draining itself
// de-asserts the level-triggered condition; this is a defensive
// clear of any stale raw status, same pattern kernel_main.vani's own
// irq_dispatch already uses).
extern "C" fn shell_rx_push_char(c: u32) -> u32;
#[no_mangle]
fn rpi4_handle_uart_rx_irq() -> i64 {
  for i from 0 to 128 {
    let fr: u32 = mmio_read_u32(UART_FR_RPI4);
    if (fr & (0x10 as u32)) == (0 as u32) {
      let c: u32 = mmio_read_u32(UART_DR_RPI4) & (0xFF as u32);
      let _ = uart_putc_rpi4_vani(c as i64);
      let _ = shell_rx_push_char(c);
    }
  }
  let _ = mmio_write_u32(UART_ICR_RPI4, 0x10 as u32);
  return 0;
}

// Round 78 -- the smallest possible proof of an ARMv8-A context
// switch (boot/rpi4/task_switch.S's own header comment has the full
// design). Deliberately cooperative (voluntary-yield), not
// interrupt-driven -- proves the "two independent stacks can be
// swapped between correctly" primitive on its own, separate from the
// already-delicate timer-IRQ path, before any real (preemptive,
// priority-based) scheduler attempts to build on top of it. NOT a
// port of kernel_main.vani's own scheduler -- that stays its own
// separate, much larger future increment.
extern "C" fn task_switch(save_to: mut ref i64, load_from: mut ref i64) -> i64;
extern "C" fn task_a_stack_init() -> i64;
extern "C" fn task_b_stack_init() -> i64;
extern "C" fn task_a_sp_addr() -> mut ref i64;
extern "C" fn task_b_sp_addr() -> mut ref i64;
extern "C" fn boot_sp_addr() -> mut ref i64;
extern "C" fn switch_count_increment() -> i64;

// Must never actually `ret` -- the fake frame task_a_stack_init built
// has no real caller below it, so the only way out is another
// task_switch call. Prints "A", then either yields to task_b (rounds
// 1-2) or switches straight back to the boot context (round 3),
// ending the demo -- task_b never needs its own termination logic
// this way, it always just yields back to A.
#[no_mangle]
fn task_a_vani_entry() -> i64 {
  while true {
    let _ = uart_puts_rpi4_vani("A");
    let count: i64 = switch_count_increment();
    let a_sp: mut ref i64 = task_a_sp_addr();
    if count >= 3 {
      let boot_sp: mut ref i64 = boot_sp_addr();
      let _ = task_switch(a_sp, boot_sp);
    } else {
      let b_sp: mut ref i64 = task_b_sp_addr();
      let _ = task_switch(a_sp, b_sp);
    }
  }
  return 0;
}

#[no_mangle]
fn task_b_vani_entry() -> i64 {
  while true {
    let _ = uart_puts_rpi4_vani("B");
    let a_sp: mut ref i64 = task_a_sp_addr();
    let b_sp: mut ref i64 = task_b_sp_addr();
    let _ = task_switch(b_sp, a_sp);
  }
  return 0;
}

// Builds both tasks' initial stacks, then switches from the boot
// context into task_a. Execution resumes at the line right after
// that call ONLY once task_a's 3rd round switches back to boot_sp --
// which cannot happen unless every switch in the whole A/B/A/B/A
// chain (5 switches total between the two tasks, plus this call and
// the final return, 7 in all) landed correctly, since a broken switch
// crashes or hangs rather than resuming at the wrong place with
// merely wrong output. The printed "ABABA" sequence is the same
// proof from the data side: exactly 3 A's and 2 B's, strictly
// alternating.
fn task_switch_self_test() -> i64 {
  let _ = task_a_stack_init();
  let _ = task_b_stack_init();
  let _ = uart_puts_rpi4_vani("DHRUVA RPI4: task-switch self-test: ");
  let boot_sp: mut ref i64 = boot_sp_addr();
  let a_sp: mut ref i64 = task_a_sp_addr();
  let _ = task_switch(boot_sp, a_sp);
  let _ = uart_puts_rpi4_vani(" (PASS)\n");
  return 0;
}

// Round 81 -- a genuine VOLUNTARY switch through the exact same
// eret-based interrupt-shaped frame rounds 79/80 use for timer-driven
// preemption (boot/rpi4/preempt_switch.S's own preempt_generic_switch
// comment has the full design) -- proves voluntary and involuntary
// switching are interoperable through one shared mechanism, not two
// disconnected ones. Bounded and self-contained, same shape as round
// 78's own "ABABA" proof: task_x yields to task_y 3 times, then on
// its 3rd round yields back to the driver (this self-test) instead,
// ending the demo. task_y never independently decides to stop,
// always yields straight back to task_x -- same asymmetric-driver
// pattern round 78's task_a/task_b already established.
extern "C" fn preempt_generic_switch(save_slot: mut ref i64, load_slot: mut ref i64) -> i64;
extern "C" fn yv_init() -> i64;
extern "C" fn yv_x_sp_addr() -> mut ref i64;
extern "C" fn yv_y_sp_addr() -> mut ref i64;
extern "C" fn yv_driver_sp_addr() -> mut ref i64;

#[no_mangle]
fn preempt_yield_task_x_entry() -> i64 {
  let i: i64 = 0;
  while i < 3 {
    let _ = uart_puts_rpi4_vani("X");
    i = i + 1;
    let x_sp: mut ref i64 = yv_x_sp_addr();
    if i >= 3 {
      let driver_sp: mut ref i64 = yv_driver_sp_addr();
      let _ = preempt_generic_switch(x_sp, driver_sp);
    } else {
      let y_sp: mut ref i64 = yv_y_sp_addr();
      let _ = preempt_generic_switch(x_sp, y_sp);
    }
  }
  return 0;
}

#[no_mangle]
fn preempt_yield_task_y_entry() -> i64 {
  while true {
    let _ = uart_puts_rpi4_vani("Y");
    let y_sp: mut ref i64 = yv_y_sp_addr();
    let x_sp: mut ref i64 = yv_x_sp_addr();
    let _ = preempt_generic_switch(y_sp, x_sp);
  }
  return 0;
}

fn preempt_yield_self_test() -> i64 {
  let _ = yv_init();
  let _ = uart_puts_rpi4_vani("DHRUVA RPI4: voluntary-yield (eret-based) self-test: ");
  let driver_sp: mut ref i64 = yv_driver_sp_addr();
  let x_sp: mut ref i64 = yv_x_sp_addr();
  let _ = preempt_generic_switch(driver_sp, x_sp);
  let _ = uart_puts_rpi4_vani(" (PASS)\n");
  return 0;
}

// Round 98 -- crypto generification: SHA-256/512, field25519/X25519/
// Ed25519, and raw-key PKI moved OUT of this file entirely and into
// three standalone, hardware-agnostic kosh packages -- vendored at
// vendor/crypto_hash, vendor/curve25519, vendor/pki (each also its
// own independent GitHub repo: vani-crypto-hash, vani-curve25519,
// vani-pki) -- rather than staying duplicated per-board hand-ports.
// Rounds 85 and 94-97 wrote this code AS this file's own inline
// functions (`sha256_hash_rpi4`, `field25519_add_rpi4`, etc.); this
// round is a pure extraction with zero algorithm changes -- every
// self-test below reuses the exact same test vectors, and the 5
// `_rpi4`-suffixed self-test functions kept here are now thin
// wrappers (call the package's own unqualified self-test, print the
// same UART message every prior round already printed) so the boot
// sequence and shell dispatch below needed zero changes.
//
// Not vendored via vani.toml [deps] + name::item resolution: a real
// vani-compiler bug (index-assignment with a function-call RHS fails
// to resolve the callee inside any named scope -- BUG-234,
// vani-compiler/docs/TODO_CURRENT.md) breaks that path for any
// package whose internals use `buf[i] = helper(...)`, which SHA-256/
// 512's own compression loops do throughout. Pulled in via plain
// relative `use` instead, landing every package's functions in this
// file's own flat global namespace (unqualified, e.g. `sha256_hash`
// not `crypto_hash::sha256_hash`) -- works around the bug at the cost
// of losing collision-avoidance, acceptable here since nothing else
// in this file declares a colliding name.
//
// The three packages' own vendor/ subdirectories were deliberately
// NOT copied here (each vendors its own next-level dependency for
// independent standalone testing, e.g. vani-curve25519 vendors its
// own copy of crypto_hash) -- copying those nested copies in too
// would define sha256_hash/etc. TWICE in this file's flat namespace.
// Instead this file vendors crypto_hash/curve25519/pki each exactly
// ONCE at the top level, and curve25519's/pki's own vendored copies
// (as flattened here) `use` a sibling path one level up instead of
// their own nested vendor/ copy -- see vendor/curve25519/src/lib.vani
// and vendor/pki/src/lib.vani's own `use` lines.
use "../vendor/crypto_hash/src/lib.vani";
use "../vendor/curve25519/src/lib.vani";
use "../vendor/pki/src/lib.vani";
// Round 166: TLS 1.3 -- zero name collisions confirmed via grep first
// (same check performed before every prior vendored package).
use "../vendor/chacha20_poly1305/src/lib.vani";

fn sha256_self_test_rpi4() -> i64 {
  let ok: i64 = sha256_self_test();
  let _ = uart_puts_rpi4_vani("CRYPTO: SHA-256 vs 3 NIST/FIPS-180-4 KATs (empty, \"abc\", 2-block)");
  if ok == 1 {
    let _ = uart_puts_rpi4_vani(" (PASS)\n");
  } else {
    let _ = uart_puts_rpi4_vani(" (FAIL)\n");
  }
  return ok;
}

fn sha512_self_test_rpi4() -> i64 {
  let ok: i64 = sha512_self_test();
  let _ = uart_puts_rpi4_vani("CRYPTO: SHA-512 vs 4 FIPS-180-4 KATs (empty, \"abc\", 111-byte, 112-byte 2-block)");
  if ok == 1 {
    let _ = uart_puts_rpi4_vani(" (PASS)\n");
  } else {
    let _ = uart_puts_rpi4_vani(" (FAIL)\n");
  }
  return ok;
}

fn x25519_self_test_rpi4() -> i64 {
  let ok: i64 = x25519_self_test();
  let _ = uart_puts_rpi4_vani("CRYPTO: X25519 vs kernel_main.vani's own verified reference (base-point + DH agreement)");
  if ok == 1 {
    let _ = uart_puts_rpi4_vani(" (PASS)\n");
  } else {
    let _ = uart_puts_rpi4_vani(" (FAIL)\n");
  }
  return ok;
}

fn ed25519_self_test_rpi4() -> i64 {
  let ok: i64 = ed25519_self_test();
  let _ = uart_puts_rpi4_vani("CRYPTO: Ed25519 vs kernel_main.vani's own verified reference (pubkey+sign+verify+tamper)");
  if ok == 1 {
    let _ = uart_puts_rpi4_vani(" (PASS)\n");
  } else {
    let _ = uart_puts_rpi4_vani(" (FAIL)\n");
  }
  return ok;
}

fn pki_self_test_rpi4() -> i64 {
  let ok: i64 = pki_self_test();
  let _ = uart_puts_rpi4_vani("CRYPTO: PKI raw public-key trust vs kernel_main.vani's own verified reference (genuine sig + tamper reject)");
  if ok == 1 {
    let _ = uart_puts_rpi4_vani(" (PASS)\n");
  } else {
    let _ = uart_puts_rpi4_vani(" (FAIL)\n");
  }
  return ok;
}

// ==================================================================
// Round 166 (TLS 1.3 port to Pi 4/5), Phase 1: pure protocol/crypto-
// orchestration logic -- record layer, key schedule, handshake
// message builders -- no scratch-buffer/heap dependency at all,
// everything threaded through function parameters/returns as fixed-
// size local arrays, matching this board's own established heap-free
// style (curve25519/chacha20_poly1305's own array-based return-by-
// value convention). Faithful port of kernel_main.vani's own TLS 1.3
// implementation (TLS_CHACHA20_POLY1305_SHA256 / X25519 / Ed25519 /
// RFC 7250 raw public keys) -- every wire-format constant, HKDF
// label, and message-building byte sequence below is copied directly
// from that file, not re-derived. The STATEFUL live-transport
// orchestration (tls_service/tls_connect/tls_accept/tls_send/
// tls_recv, needing session state to persist across many separate
// poll/call cycles) is Phase 2, a separate addition needing its own
// new boot/rpi4/tls13_scratch.S persistent-storage file.
// ==================================================================

// --- Wire-format helpers ---

fn tls_write_u8_rpi4(buf: mut ref [u8; 512], pos: i64, v: i64) -> i64 {
  buf[pos] = (v & 255) as u8;
  return pos + 1;
}

fn tls_write_u16_rpi4(buf: mut ref [u8; 512], pos: i64, v: i64) -> i64 {
  buf[pos] = ((v >> 8) & 255) as u8;
  buf[pos + 1] = (v & 255) as u8;
  return pos + 2;
}

fn tls_write_u24_rpi4(buf: mut ref [u8; 512], pos: i64, v: i64) -> i64 {
  buf[pos] = ((v >> 16) & 255) as u8;
  buf[pos + 1] = ((v >> 8) & 255) as u8;
  buf[pos + 2] = (v & 255) as u8;
  return pos + 3;
}

fn tls_write_bytes32_rpi4(buf: mut ref [u8; 512], pos: i64, src: ref [u8; 32]) -> i64 {
  let i: i64 = 0;
  while i < 32 {
    buf[pos + i] = src[i];
    i = i + 1;
  }
  return pos + 32;
}

fn tls_write_bytes64_rpi4(buf: mut ref [u8; 512], pos: i64, src: ref [u8; 64]) -> i64 {
  let i: i64 = 0;
  while i < 64 {
    buf[pos + i] = src[i];
    i = i + 1;
  }
  return pos + 64;
}

fn tls_basepoint32_rpi4() -> [u8; 32] {
  let out: [u8; 32] = [0 as u8; 32];
  out[0] = 9 as u8;
  return out;
}

fn tls_bytes_equal32_rpi4(a: ref [u8; 32], b: ref [u8; 32]) -> i64 {
  let i: i64 = 0;
  while i < 32 {
    if a[i] != b[i] {
      return 0;
    }
    i = i + 1;
  }
  return 1;
}

// General-purpose N-byte compare for record content-round-trip
// checks (record/message lengths vary; the 32-byte-fixed
// tls_bytes_equal32_rpi4 above only covers key/hash-shaped compares).
fn tls_bytes_equal512_rpi4(a: ref [u8; 512], b: ref [u8; 512], n: i64) -> i64 {
  let i: i64 = 0;
  while i < n {
    if a[i] != b[i] {
      return 0;
    }
    i = i + 1;
  }
  return 1;
}

// --- Handshake message builders (RFC 8446 4.1.2/4.1.3/4.3.1/4.3.2/
// 4.4.4) -- wire layout and byte counts copied directly from
// kernel_main.vani's own tls_build_* functions (themselves derived
// from that round's own tls13_ref.py). ---

fn tls_build_client_hello_rpi4(client_random: ref [u8; 32], client_pub: ref [u8; 32], out: mut ref [u8; 512]) -> i64 {
  let o: i64 = 0;
  o = tls_write_u8_rpi4(out, o, 1);
  o = tls_write_u24_rpi4(out, o, 108);
  o = tls_write_u16_rpi4(out, o, 0x0303);
  o = tls_write_bytes32_rpi4(out, o, client_random);
  o = tls_write_u8_rpi4(out, o, 0);
  o = tls_write_u16_rpi4(out, o, 2);
  o = tls_write_u16_rpi4(out, o, 0x1303);
  o = tls_write_u8_rpi4(out, o, 1);
  o = tls_write_u8_rpi4(out, o, 0);
  o = tls_write_u16_rpi4(out, o, 65);
  o = tls_write_u16_rpi4(out, o, 43);
  o = tls_write_u16_rpi4(out, o, 3);
  o = tls_write_u8_rpi4(out, o, 2);
  o = tls_write_u16_rpi4(out, o, 0x0304);
  o = tls_write_u16_rpi4(out, o, 10);
  o = tls_write_u16_rpi4(out, o, 4);
  o = tls_write_u16_rpi4(out, o, 2);
  o = tls_write_u16_rpi4(out, o, 0x001D);
  o = tls_write_u16_rpi4(out, o, 13);
  o = tls_write_u16_rpi4(out, o, 4);
  o = tls_write_u16_rpi4(out, o, 2);
  o = tls_write_u16_rpi4(out, o, 0x0807);
  o = tls_write_u16_rpi4(out, o, 51);
  o = tls_write_u16_rpi4(out, o, 38);
  o = tls_write_u16_rpi4(out, o, 36);
  o = tls_write_u16_rpi4(out, o, 0x001D);
  o = tls_write_u16_rpi4(out, o, 32);
  o = tls_write_bytes32_rpi4(out, o, client_pub);
  return o;
}

fn tls_build_server_hello_rpi4(server_random: ref [u8; 32], server_pub: ref [u8; 32], out: mut ref [u8; 512]) -> i64 {
  let o: i64 = 0;
  o = tls_write_u8_rpi4(out, o, 2);
  o = tls_write_u24_rpi4(out, o, 86);
  o = tls_write_u16_rpi4(out, o, 0x0303);
  o = tls_write_bytes32_rpi4(out, o, server_random);
  o = tls_write_u8_rpi4(out, o, 0);
  o = tls_write_u16_rpi4(out, o, 0x1303);
  o = tls_write_u8_rpi4(out, o, 0);
  o = tls_write_u16_rpi4(out, o, 46);
  o = tls_write_u16_rpi4(out, o, 43);
  o = tls_write_u16_rpi4(out, o, 2);
  o = tls_write_u16_rpi4(out, o, 0x0304);
  o = tls_write_u16_rpi4(out, o, 51);
  o = tls_write_u16_rpi4(out, o, 36);
  o = tls_write_u16_rpi4(out, o, 0x001D);
  o = tls_write_u16_rpi4(out, o, 32);
  o = tls_write_bytes32_rpi4(out, o, server_pub);
  return o;
}

fn tls_build_encrypted_extensions_rpi4(out: mut ref [u8; 512]) -> i64 {
  let o: i64 = 0;
  o = tls_write_u8_rpi4(out, o, 8);
  o = tls_write_u24_rpi4(out, o, 2);
  o = tls_write_u16_rpi4(out, o, 0);
  return o;
}

fn tls_build_certificate_rpi4(raw_pubkey: ref [u8; 32], out: mut ref [u8; 512]) -> i64 {
  let o: i64 = 0;
  o = tls_write_u8_rpi4(out, o, 11);
  o = tls_write_u24_rpi4(out, o, 41);
  o = tls_write_u8_rpi4(out, o, 0);
  o = tls_write_u24_rpi4(out, o, 37);
  o = tls_write_u24_rpi4(out, o, 32);
  o = tls_write_bytes32_rpi4(out, o, raw_pubkey);
  o = tls_write_u16_rpi4(out, o, 0);
  return o;
}

fn tls_build_certificate_verify_rpi4(sig: ref [u8; 64], out: mut ref [u8; 512]) -> i64 {
  let o: i64 = 0;
  o = tls_write_u8_rpi4(out, o, 15);
  o = tls_write_u24_rpi4(out, o, 68);
  o = tls_write_u16_rpi4(out, o, 0x0807);
  o = tls_write_u16_rpi4(out, o, 64);
  o = tls_write_bytes64_rpi4(out, o, sig);
  return o;
}

fn tls_build_finished_rpi4(verify_data: ref [u8; 32], out: mut ref [u8; 512]) -> i64 {
  let o: i64 = 0;
  o = tls_write_u8_rpi4(out, o, 20);
  o = tls_write_u24_rpi4(out, o, 32);
  o = tls_write_bytes32_rpi4(out, o, verify_data);
  return o;
}

// RFC 8446 4.4.3: 64 spaces || context string || 0x00 || transcript hash.
fn tls_certverify_signed_content_rpi4(transcript_hash: ref [u8; 32], out: mut ref [u8; 512]) -> i64 {
  let o: i64 = 0;
  let pad_i: i64 = 0;
  while pad_i < 64 {
    out[o] = 0x20 as u8;
    o = o + 1;
    pad_i = pad_i + 1;
  }
  let ctx: Str = "TLS 1.3, server CertificateVerify";
  let ctx_len: i64 = str_len_bytes(ctx);
  let ctx_i: i64 = 0;
  while ctx_i < ctx_len {
    out[o + ctx_i] = str_byte_at(ctx, ctx_i) as u8;
    ctx_i = ctx_i + 1;
  }
  o = o + ctx_len;
  o = tls_write_u8_rpi4(out, o, 0);
  o = tls_write_bytes32_rpi4(out, o, transcript_hash);
  return o;
}

// --- HMAC-SHA256 / HKDF (RFC 2104 / RFC 5869) ---
// Single HMAC implementation (unlike kernel_main.vani's own two,
// which stayed separate there purely to avoid touching an already-
// shipped narrower primitive) -- this is a fresh Pi4/5 port, so one
// 256-byte-capped HMAC covers every real call site here (every actual
// message HMAC-SHA256 hashes in this file's own key schedule is well
// under 256 bytes -- see tls_build_hkdf_label_rpi4's own comment).
// Every TLS 1.3 key-schedule secret/PRK is always exactly 32 bytes
// (a SHA-256 output), so the ">64-byte key, hash it down first"
// branch kernel_main.vani's own general-purpose hmac_sha256 also
// needs is never reachable here and is deliberately not ported.
fn tls_hmac_sha256_rpi4(key: ref [u8; 32], msg: ref [u8; 256], msg_len: i64) -> [u8; 32] {
  let key_block: [u8; 256] = [0 as u8; 256];
  let i: i64 = 0;
  while i < 32 {
    key_block[i] = key[i];
    i = i + 1;
  }

  let msg_scratch: [u8; 256] = [0 as u8; 256];
  i = 0;
  while i < 64 {
    msg_scratch[i] = key_block[i] ^ (0x36 as u8);
    i = i + 1;
  }
  i = 0;
  while i < msg_len {
    msg_scratch[64 + i] = msg[i];
    i = i + 1;
  }
  let inner_hash: [u8; 32] = [0 as u8; 32];
  let _ = sha256_hash(mut ref msg_scratch, 64 + msg_len, mut ref inner_hash);

  let msg_scratch2: [u8; 256] = [0 as u8; 256];
  i = 0;
  while i < 64 {
    msg_scratch2[i] = key_block[i] ^ (0x5c as u8);
    i = i + 1;
  }
  i = 0;
  while i < 32 {
    msg_scratch2[64 + i] = inner_hash[i];
    i = i + 1;
  }
  let out: [u8; 32] = [0 as u8; 32];
  let _ = sha256_hash(mut ref msg_scratch2, 96, mut ref out);
  return out;
}

fn tls_hkdf_extract_rpi4(salt: ref [u8; 32], ikm: ref [u8; 32]) -> [u8; 32] {
  let ikm_buf: [u8; 256] = [0 as u8; 256];
  let i: i64 = 0;
  while i < 32 {
    ikm_buf[i] = ikm[i];
    i = i + 1;
  }
  return tls_hmac_sha256_rpi4(salt, ref ikm_buf, 32);
}

struct TlsHkdfLabelResult { buf: [u8; 512], span_len: i64 }

// HkdfLabel struct (RFC 8446 7.1): 2-byte length, length-prefixed
// "tls13 "+label, length-prefixed context. Max realistic size here:
// 2+1+(6+13)+1+32 = 55 bytes ("s ap traffic" is the longest label
// used) -- comfortably fits the 256-byte HMAC message cap this file's
// own tls_hmac_sha256_rpi4 assumes.
fn tls_build_hkdf_label_rpi4(label: Str, context: ref [u8; 32], context_len: i64, length: i64) -> TlsHkdfLabelResult {
  let buf: [u8; 512] = [0 as u8; 512];
  let o: i64 = 0;
  o = tls_write_u16_rpi4(mut ref buf, o, length);
  let prefix: Str = "tls13 ";
  let prefix_len: i64 = str_len_bytes(prefix);
  let label_len: i64 = str_len_bytes(label);
  let full_label_len: i64 = prefix_len + label_len;
  o = tls_write_u8_rpi4(mut ref buf, o, full_label_len);
  let pfx_i: i64 = 0;
  while pfx_i < prefix_len {
    buf[o + pfx_i] = str_byte_at(prefix, pfx_i) as u8;
    pfx_i = pfx_i + 1;
  }
  o = o + prefix_len;
  let lbl_i: i64 = 0;
  while lbl_i < label_len {
    buf[o + lbl_i] = str_byte_at(label, lbl_i) as u8;
    lbl_i = lbl_i + 1;
  }
  o = o + label_len;
  o = tls_write_u8_rpi4(mut ref buf, o, context_len);
  let ci: i64 = 0;
  while ci < context_len {
    buf[o + ci] = context[ci];
    ci = ci + 1;
  }
  o = o + context_len;
  return TlsHkdfLabelResult { buf: buf, span_len: o };
}

// HKDF-Expand (RFC 5869 2.3): T(0)=empty, T(i)=HMAC(PRK, T(i-1) ||
// info || i), OKM = T(1)||T(2)||... truncated to out_len. out_len is
// always 12 or 32 here (an IV or a key/secret), so a 32-byte output
// buffer always has enough room.
fn tls_hkdf_expand_rpi4(prk: ref [u8; 32], hklabel: ref [u8; 512], hklabel_len: i64, out_len: i64) -> [u8; 32] {
  let t: [u8; 32] = [0 as u8; 32];
  let t_len: i64 = 0;
  let out: [u8; 32] = [0 as u8; 32];
  let produced: i64 = 0;
  let counter: i64 = 1;
  while produced < out_len {
    let hmac_input: [u8; 256] = [0 as u8; 256];
    let i: i64 = 0;
    while i < t_len {
      hmac_input[i] = t[i];
      i = i + 1;
    }
    let j: i64 = 0;
    while j < hklabel_len {
      hmac_input[t_len + j] = hklabel[j];
      j = j + 1;
    }
    hmac_input[t_len + hklabel_len] = counter as u8;
    let input_len: i64 = t_len + hklabel_len + 1;

    t = tls_hmac_sha256_rpi4(prk, ref hmac_input, input_len);
    t_len = 32;

    let take: i64 = if (out_len - produced) < 32 { out_len - produced } else { 32 };
    let k: i64 = 0;
    while k < take {
      out[produced + k] = t[k];
      k = k + 1;
    }
    produced = produced + take;
    counter = counter + 1;
  }
  return out;
}

fn tls_hkdf_expand_label_rpi4(secret: ref [u8; 32], label: Str, context: ref [u8; 32], context_len: i64, length: i64) -> [u8; 32] {
  let hl: TlsHkdfLabelResult = tls_build_hkdf_label_rpi4(label, context, context_len, length);
  let hl_bytes: [u8; 512] = hl.buf;
  let hl_len: i64 = hl.span_len;
  return tls_hkdf_expand_rpi4(secret, ref hl_bytes, hl_len, length);
}

// --- Transcript hashing (streams through crypto_hash's own
// Sha256Ctx, since the transcript can exceed sha256_hash's own
// 256-byte one-shot cap once several handshake messages have
// accumulated) ---

// Hand-unrolled into up to 4 distinctly-named Sha256Ctx bindings
// (ctx0 -> ctx1 -> ctx2 -> ctx3 -> ctx4) rather than a loop that
// reassigns a single `ctx` variable to itself (`ctx = sha256_update
// (ref ctx, ...)`) -- deliberately avoiding that self-reassignment-
// in-a-loop shape after it was found to corrupt unrelated memory on
// this board (round 166: a live boot regression where enabling this
// function broke tcp_conn_self_test_rpi4 much later in the same boot
// sequence, root-caused to this exact pattern -- crypto_hash's own
// `sha256_streaming_self_test` never does `ctxN = sha256_update(ref
// ctxN, ...)`, only ever chaining into a FRESH name per step, and
// matching that shape here fixed it). 4 chunks of 256 bytes covers
// every real transcript this file ever builds (max ~400 bytes through
// server Finished) with comfortable headroom up to 1024.
fn tls_hash_transcript_rpi4(transcript: ref [u8; 1024], transcript_len: i64) -> [u8; 32] {
  let ctx0: Sha256Ctx = sha256_init();
  let chunk0: [u8; 256] = [0 as u8; 256];
  let take0: i64 = if transcript_len < 256 { transcript_len } else { 256 };
  let i: i64 = 0;
  while i < take0 { chunk0[i] = transcript[i]; i = i + 1; }
  let ctx1: Sha256Ctx = sha256_update(ref ctx0, mut ref chunk0, take0);
  if transcript_len <= 256 {
    return sha256_finalize(ref ctx1);
  }

  let chunk1: [u8; 256] = [0 as u8; 256];
  let take1: i64 = if (transcript_len - 256) < 256 { transcript_len - 256 } else { 256 };
  i = 0;
  while i < take1 { chunk1[i] = transcript[256 + i]; i = i + 1; }
  let ctx2: Sha256Ctx = sha256_update(ref ctx1, mut ref chunk1, take1);
  if transcript_len <= 512 {
    return sha256_finalize(ref ctx2);
  }

  let chunk2: [u8; 256] = [0 as u8; 256];
  let take2: i64 = if (transcript_len - 512) < 256 { transcript_len - 512 } else { 256 };
  i = 0;
  while i < take2 { chunk2[i] = transcript[512 + i]; i = i + 1; }
  let ctx3: Sha256Ctx = sha256_update(ref ctx2, mut ref chunk2, take2);
  if transcript_len <= 768 {
    return sha256_finalize(ref ctx3);
  }

  let chunk3: [u8; 256] = [0 as u8; 256];
  let take3: i64 = if (transcript_len - 768) < 256 { transcript_len - 768 } else { 256 };
  i = 0;
  while i < take3 { chunk3[i] = transcript[768 + i]; i = i + 1; }
  let ctx4: Sha256Ctx = sha256_update(ref ctx3, mut ref chunk3, take3);
  return sha256_finalize(ref ctx4);
}

// Derive-Secret(Secret, Label, Messages) = HKDF-Expand-Label(Secret,
// Label, Transcript-Hash(Messages), Hash.length). transcript_len=0 is
// a real, meaningful case (Transcript-Hash of the empty message, used
// by the "derived" label) -- NOT a no-op skip.
fn tls_derive_secret_rpi4(secret: ref [u8; 32], label: Str, transcript: ref [u8; 1024], transcript_len: i64) -> [u8; 32] {
  let th: [u8; 32] = tls_hash_transcript_rpi4(transcript, transcript_len);
  return tls_hkdf_expand_label_rpi4(secret, label, ref th, 32, 32);
}

struct TlsTrafficKeys { key: [u8; 32], iv: [u8; 12] }

fn tls_derive_traffic_keys_rpi4(secret: ref [u8; 32]) -> TlsTrafficKeys {
  let dummy: [u8; 32] = [0 as u8; 32];
  let key32: [u8; 32] = tls_hkdf_expand_label_rpi4(secret, "key", ref dummy, 0, 32);
  let iv32: [u8; 32] = tls_hkdf_expand_label_rpi4(secret, "iv", ref dummy, 0, 12);
  let iv: [u8; 12] = [0 as u8; 12];
  let i: i64 = 0;
  while i < 12 {
    iv[i] = iv32[i];
    i = i + 1;
  }
  return TlsTrafficKeys { key: key32, iv: iv };
}

// finished_key = HKDF-Expand-Label(BaseKey, "finished", "", 32);
// verify_data = HMAC(finished_key, Transcript-Hash(Messages)).
fn tls_finished_verify_data_rpi4(traffic_secret: ref [u8; 32], transcript: ref [u8; 1024], transcript_len: i64) -> [u8; 32] {
  let dummy: [u8; 32] = [0 as u8; 32];
  let fk: [u8; 32] = tls_hkdf_expand_label_rpi4(traffic_secret, "finished", ref dummy, 0, 32);
  let th: [u8; 32] = tls_hash_transcript_rpi4(transcript, transcript_len);
  let th_buf: [u8; 256] = [0 as u8; 256];
  let i: i64 = 0;
  while i < 32 {
    th_buf[i] = th[i];
    i = i + 1;
  }
  return tls_hmac_sha256_rpi4(ref fk, ref th_buf, 32);
}

// --- Record layer (RFC 8446 5.2/5.3) ---

// nonce = static_iv XOR (seq_num, big-endian, zero-padded to 12
// bytes).
fn tls_record_nonce_rpi4(static_iv: ref [u8; 12], seq: i64) -> [u8; 12] {
  let out: [u8; 12] = [0 as u8; 12];
  let i: i64 = 0;
  while i < 8 {
    out[i] = static_iv[i];
    i = i + 1;
  }
  let b3: i64 = (seq >> 24) & 255;
  let b2: i64 = (seq >> 16) & 255;
  let b1: i64 = (seq >> 8) & 255;
  let b0: i64 = seq & 255;
  out[8] = ((static_iv[8] as i64) ^ b3) as u8;
  out[9] = ((static_iv[9] as i64) ^ b2) as u8;
  out[10] = ((static_iv[10] as i64) ^ b1) as u8;
  out[11] = ((static_iv[11] as i64) ^ b0) as u8;
  return out;
}

struct TlsEncryptResult { rec: [u8; 512], span_len: i64 }

// TLSInnerPlaintext = content || real_content_type (no padding); AAD
// = the 5-byte TLSCiphertext header. Returns the total rec
// (5-byte header + ciphertext + 16-byte tag).
fn tls_encrypt_record_rpi4(key: ref [u8; 32], static_iv: ref [u8; 12], seq: i64, content: ref [u8; 512], content_len: i64, content_type: i64) -> TlsEncryptResult {
  let inner: [u8; 512] = [0 as u8; 512];
  let i: i64 = 0;
  while i < content_len {
    inner[i] = content[i];
    i = i + 1;
  }
  inner[content_len] = content_type as u8;
  let inner_len: i64 = content_len + 1;

  let record_len_field: i64 = inner_len + 16;
  let aad: [u8; 64] = [0 as u8; 64];
  aad[0] = 0x17 as u8;
  aad[1] = 0x03 as u8;
  aad[2] = 0x03 as u8;
  aad[3] = ((record_len_field / 256) & 255) as u8;
  aad[4] = (record_len_field & 255) as u8;

  let nonce: [u8; 12] = tls_record_nonce_rpi4(static_iv, seq);
  let enc: AeadEncryptResult = chacha20_poly1305_encrypt(key, ref nonce, ref aad, 5, ref inner, inner_len);

  let out: [u8; 512] = [0 as u8; 512];
  let j: i64 = 0;
  while j < 5 {
    out[j] = aad[j];
    j = j + 1;
  }
  let ct: [u8; 512] = enc.ciphertext;
  let tag: [u8; 16] = enc.tag;
  j = 0;
  while j < inner_len {
    out[5 + j] = ct[j];
    j = j + 1;
  }
  j = 0;
  while j < 16 {
    out[5 + inner_len + j] = tag[j];
    j = j + 1;
  }
  return TlsEncryptResult { rec: out, span_len: 5 + inner_len + 16 };
}

struct TlsDecryptResult { ok: i64, content: [u8; 512], span_len: i64 }

// Decrypts `rec` (record_len bytes) and verifies its inner content
// type equals `expected_content_type` -- reject, don't guess.
fn tls_decrypt_record_rpi4(key: ref [u8; 32], static_iv: ref [u8; 12], seq: i64, rec_data: ref [u8; 512], record_len: i64, expected_content_type: i64) -> TlsDecryptResult {
  let ct_len: i64 = record_len - 5 - 16;
  let empty: [u8; 512] = [0 as u8; 512];
  if ct_len < 0 {
    return TlsDecryptResult { ok: 0, content: empty, span_len: 0 };
  }
  let aad: [u8; 64] = [0 as u8; 64];
  let i: i64 = 0;
  while i < 5 {
    aad[i] = rec_data[i];
    i = i + 1;
  }
  let ct_scratch: [u8; 512] = [0 as u8; 512];
  i = 0;
  while i < ct_len {
    ct_scratch[i] = rec_data[5 + i];
    i = i + 1;
  }
  let tag_in: [u8; 16] = [0 as u8; 16];
  i = 0;
  while i < 16 {
    tag_in[i] = rec_data[5 + ct_len + i];
    i = i + 1;
  }

  let nonce: [u8; 12] = tls_record_nonce_rpi4(static_iv, seq);
  let dec: AeadDecryptResult = chacha20_poly1305_decrypt(key, ref nonce, ref aad, 5, ref ct_scratch, ct_len, ref tag_in);
  if dec.ok != 1 {
    return TlsDecryptResult { ok: 0, content: empty, span_len: 0 };
  }
  let plaintext: [u8; 512] = dec.plaintext;
  let content_len: i64 = ct_len - 1;
  let content_type: i64 = plaintext[content_len] as i64;
  if content_type != expected_content_type {
    return TlsDecryptResult { ok: 0, content: empty, span_len: 0 };
  }
  let out_content: [u8; 512] = [0 as u8; 512];
  i = 0;
  while i < content_len {
    out_content[i] = plaintext[i];
    i = i + 1;
  }
  return TlsDecryptResult { ok: 1, content: out_content, span_len: content_len };
}

// Live, both-roles TLS 1.3 handshake + application-data round trip
// (TLS_CHACHA20_POLY1305_SHA256 / x25519 / ed25519 / RFC 7250 raw
// public keys). Same deterministic test vectors as kernel_main.vani's
// own tls_self_test, so this is directly comparable against that
// board's own verified reference.
//
// Stack-economy note: interleaves each message's build -> transcript-
// copy -> (round-trip encrypt/decrypt verify) into one phase before
// moving to the next, reusing a small handful of shared [u8;512]/
// TlsEncryptResult/TlsDecryptResult scratch locals across every phase
// instead of giving each message/round-trip its own distinctly-named
// buffer. This is a pure reordering (build-then-verify-immediately
// instead of build-everything-then-verify-everything) with no
// semantic effect on what's being tested. Same stack-budget lesson as
// round 109's own mqttecho fix on Pi 1 (a naive first draft with ~20
// distinct [u8;512]-shaped locals live at once overflowed this
// board's own 16KB boot stack -- boot/rpi4/link.ld's own comment --
// silently, with no fault message, since Pi 4/5 has no bounded-stack
// checker at all to catch this at compile time the way Pi 1's
// #[bounded_stack] annotations do).
fn tls_self_test_rpi4() -> i64 {
  let ok: i64 = 1;

  let zero32: [u8; 32] = [0 as u8; 32];
  let empty_transcript: [u8; 1024] = [0 as u8; 1024];

  let client_random: [u8; 32] = [
    0x01 as u8, 0x04 as u8, 0x07 as u8, 0x0a as u8, 0x0d as u8, 0x10 as u8, 0x13 as u8, 0x16 as u8,
    0x19 as u8, 0x1c as u8, 0x1f as u8, 0x22 as u8, 0x25 as u8, 0x28 as u8, 0x2b as u8, 0x2e as u8,
    0x31 as u8, 0x34 as u8, 0x37 as u8, 0x3a as u8, 0x3d as u8, 0x40 as u8, 0x43 as u8, 0x46 as u8,
    0x49 as u8, 0x4c as u8, 0x4f as u8, 0x52 as u8, 0x55 as u8, 0x58 as u8, 0x5b as u8, 0x5e as u8
  ];
  let server_random: [u8; 32] = [
    0x02 as u8, 0x07 as u8, 0x0c as u8, 0x11 as u8, 0x16 as u8, 0x1b as u8, 0x20 as u8, 0x25 as u8,
    0x2a as u8, 0x2f as u8, 0x34 as u8, 0x39 as u8, 0x3e as u8, 0x43 as u8, 0x48 as u8, 0x4d as u8,
    0x52 as u8, 0x57 as u8, 0x5c as u8, 0x61 as u8, 0x66 as u8, 0x6b as u8, 0x70 as u8, 0x75 as u8,
    0x7a as u8, 0x7f as u8, 0x84 as u8, 0x89 as u8, 0x8e as u8, 0x93 as u8, 0x98 as u8, 0x9d as u8
  ];
  let client_priv: [u8; 32] = [
    0x03 as u8, 0x0a as u8, 0x11 as u8, 0x18 as u8, 0x1f as u8, 0x26 as u8, 0x2d as u8, 0x34 as u8,
    0x3b as u8, 0x42 as u8, 0x49 as u8, 0x50 as u8, 0x57 as u8, 0x5e as u8, 0x65 as u8, 0x6c as u8,
    0x73 as u8, 0x7a as u8, 0x81 as u8, 0x88 as u8, 0x8f as u8, 0x96 as u8, 0x9d as u8, 0xa4 as u8,
    0xab as u8, 0xb2 as u8, 0xb9 as u8, 0xc0 as u8, 0xc7 as u8, 0xce as u8, 0xd5 as u8, 0xdc as u8
  ];
  let server_priv: [u8; 32] = [
    0x04 as u8, 0x0f as u8, 0x1a as u8, 0x25 as u8, 0x30 as u8, 0x3b as u8, 0x46 as u8, 0x51 as u8,
    0x5c as u8, 0x67 as u8, 0x72 as u8, 0x7d as u8, 0x88 as u8, 0x93 as u8, 0x9e as u8, 0xa9 as u8,
    0xb4 as u8, 0xbf as u8, 0xca as u8, 0xd5 as u8, 0xe0 as u8, 0xeb as u8, 0xf6 as u8, 0x01 as u8,
    0x0c as u8, 0x17 as u8, 0x22 as u8, 0x2d as u8, 0x38 as u8, 0x43 as u8, 0x4e as u8, 0x59 as u8
  ];
  let server_id_priv: [u8; 32] = [
    0x05 as u8, 0x12 as u8, 0x1f as u8, 0x2c as u8, 0x39 as u8, 0x46 as u8, 0x53 as u8, 0x60 as u8,
    0x6d as u8, 0x7a as u8, 0x87 as u8, 0x94 as u8, 0xa1 as u8, 0xae as u8, 0xbb as u8, 0xc8 as u8,
    0xd5 as u8, 0xe2 as u8, 0xef as u8, 0xfc as u8, 0x09 as u8, 0x16 as u8, 0x23 as u8, 0x30 as u8,
    0x3d as u8, 0x4a as u8, 0x57 as u8, 0x64 as u8, 0x71 as u8, 0x7e as u8, 0x8b as u8, 0x98 as u8
  ];

  let basepoint: [u8; 32] = tls_basepoint32_rpi4();
  let client_pub: [u8; 32] = x25519_scalarmult(ref client_priv, ref basepoint);
  let server_pub: [u8; 32] = x25519_scalarmult(ref server_priv, ref basepoint);

  let shared_c: [u8; 32] = x25519_scalarmult(ref client_priv, ref server_pub);
  let shared_s: [u8; 32] = x25519_scalarmult(ref server_priv, ref client_pub);
  if tls_bytes_equal32_rpi4(ref shared_c, ref shared_s) != 1 { ok = 0; }

  let server_id_pub: [u8; 32] = ed25519_secret_to_public(ref server_id_priv);

  // Shared scratch reused across every message-build + round-trip-
  // verify phase below.
  let msg_buf: [u8; 512] = [0 as u8; 512];
  let rec_buf: [u8; 512] = [0 as u8; 512];
  let recv_len: i64 = 0;
  let transcript: [u8; 1024] = [0 as u8; 1024];
  let t_len: i64 = 0;
  let ti: i64 = 0;

  // --- ClientHello ---
  let ch_len: i64 = tls_build_client_hello_rpi4(ref client_random, ref client_pub, mut ref msg_buf);
  ti = 0;
  while ti < ch_len { transcript[t_len + ti] = msg_buf[ti]; ti = ti + 1; }
  t_len = t_len + ch_len;

  // --- ServerHello ---
  let sh_len: i64 = tls_build_server_hello_rpi4(ref server_random, ref server_pub, mut ref msg_buf);
  ti = 0;
  while ti < sh_len { transcript[t_len + ti] = msg_buf[ti]; ti = ti + 1; }
  t_len = t_len + sh_len;
  let t_len_sh: i64 = t_len;

  // --- Key schedule through handshake traffic secrets ---
  let early_secret: [u8; 32] = tls_hkdf_extract_rpi4(ref zero32, ref zero32);
  let derived_es: [u8; 32] = tls_derive_secret_rpi4(ref early_secret, "derived", ref empty_transcript, 0);
  let handshake_secret: [u8; 32] = tls_hkdf_extract_rpi4(ref derived_es, ref shared_c);

  let c_hs_traffic: [u8; 32] = tls_derive_secret_rpi4(ref handshake_secret, "c hs traffic", ref transcript, t_len_sh);
  let s_hs_traffic: [u8; 32] = tls_derive_secret_rpi4(ref handshake_secret, "s hs traffic", ref transcript, t_len_sh);

  let c_hs_keys: TlsTrafficKeys = tls_derive_traffic_keys_rpi4(ref c_hs_traffic);
  let c_hs_key: [u8; 32] = c_hs_keys.key;
  let c_hs_iv: [u8; 12] = c_hs_keys.iv;
  let s_hs_keys: TlsTrafficKeys = tls_derive_traffic_keys_rpi4(ref s_hs_traffic);
  let s_hs_key: [u8; 32] = s_hs_keys.key;
  let s_hs_iv: [u8; 12] = s_hs_keys.iv;

  // DEBUG bisection: return early right after key-schedule derivation,
  // before any message building or record-layer encrypt/decrypt.

  // --- EncryptedExtensions: build, transcript, round-trip verify ---
  let ee_len: i64 = tls_build_encrypted_extensions_rpi4(mut ref msg_buf);
  ti = 0;
  while ti < ee_len { transcript[t_len + ti] = msg_buf[ti]; ti = ti + 1; }
  t_len = t_len + ee_len;

  let enc_ee: TlsEncryptResult = tls_encrypt_record_rpi4(ref s_hs_key, ref s_hs_iv, 0, ref msg_buf, ee_len, 22);
  rec_buf = enc_ee.rec;
  recv_len = enc_ee.span_len;
  let dec_ee: TlsDecryptResult = tls_decrypt_record_rpi4(ref s_hs_key, ref s_hs_iv, 0, ref rec_buf, recv_len, 22);
  if (dec_ee.ok != 1) || (dec_ee.span_len != ee_len) || (tls_bytes_equal512_rpi4(ref dec_ee.content, ref msg_buf, ee_len) != 1) { ok = 0; }

  // --- Certificate: build, transcript, cert_transcript_hash, round-trip verify ---
  let cert_len: i64 = tls_build_certificate_rpi4(ref server_id_pub, mut ref msg_buf);
  ti = 0;
  while ti < cert_len { transcript[t_len + ti] = msg_buf[ti]; ti = ti + 1; }
  t_len = t_len + cert_len;
  let t_len_cert: i64 = t_len;

  let cert_transcript_hash: [u8; 32] = tls_hash_transcript_rpi4(ref transcript, t_len_cert);

  let enc_cert: TlsEncryptResult = tls_encrypt_record_rpi4(ref s_hs_key, ref s_hs_iv, 1, ref msg_buf, cert_len, 22);
  rec_buf = enc_cert.rec;
  recv_len = enc_cert.span_len;
  let dec_cert: TlsDecryptResult = tls_decrypt_record_rpi4(ref s_hs_key, ref s_hs_iv, 1, ref rec_buf, recv_len, 22);
  if (dec_cert.ok != 1) || (dec_cert.span_len != cert_len) || (tls_bytes_equal512_rpi4(ref dec_cert.content, ref msg_buf, cert_len) != 1) { ok = 0; }

  // --- CertificateVerify: cv_content built into msg_buf (cert no
  // longer needed after its own check above), signed, then msg_buf
  // is overwritten with the actual CertificateVerify message built
  // from that signature (cv_content itself no longer needed once
  // cv_sig is computed). ---
  let cv_content_len: i64 = tls_certverify_signed_content_rpi4(ref cert_transcript_hash, mut ref msg_buf);
  let cv_sig: [u8; 64] = ed25519_sign(ref server_id_priv, ref msg_buf, cv_content_len);
  let cv_len: i64 = tls_build_certificate_verify_rpi4(ref cv_sig, mut ref msg_buf);
  ti = 0;
  while ti < cv_len { transcript[t_len + ti] = msg_buf[ti]; ti = ti + 1; }
  t_len = t_len + cv_len;
  let t_len_cv: i64 = t_len;

  let enc_cv: TlsEncryptResult = tls_encrypt_record_rpi4(ref s_hs_key, ref s_hs_iv, 2, ref msg_buf, cv_len, 22);
  rec_buf = enc_cv.rec;
  recv_len = enc_cv.span_len;
  let dec_cv: TlsDecryptResult = tls_decrypt_record_rpi4(ref s_hs_key, ref s_hs_iv, 2, ref rec_buf, recv_len, 22);
  if (dec_cv.ok != 1) || (dec_cv.span_len != cv_len) || (tls_bytes_equal512_rpi4(ref dec_cv.content, ref msg_buf, cv_len) != 1) { ok = 0; }

  // --- Server Finished ---
  let s_verify_data: [u8; 32] = tls_finished_verify_data_rpi4(ref s_hs_traffic, ref transcript, t_len_cv);
  let s_fin_len: i64 = tls_build_finished_rpi4(ref s_verify_data, mut ref msg_buf);
  ti = 0;
  while ti < s_fin_len { transcript[t_len + ti] = msg_buf[ti]; ti = ti + 1; }
  t_len = t_len + s_fin_len;
  let t_len_s_fin: i64 = t_len;

  let enc_s_fin: TlsEncryptResult = tls_encrypt_record_rpi4(ref s_hs_key, ref s_hs_iv, 3, ref msg_buf, s_fin_len, 22);
  rec_buf = enc_s_fin.rec;
  recv_len = enc_s_fin.span_len;
  let dec_s_fin: TlsDecryptResult = tls_decrypt_record_rpi4(ref s_hs_key, ref s_hs_iv, 3, ref rec_buf, recv_len, 22);
  if (dec_s_fin.ok != 1) || (dec_s_fin.span_len != s_fin_len) || (tls_bytes_equal512_rpi4(ref dec_s_fin.content, ref msg_buf, s_fin_len) != 1) { ok = 0; }

  // Tamper check -- rec_buf/recv_len still hold the just-built s_fin
  // record (nothing overwrote them since the round-trip check above).
  let tampered: [u8; 512] = rec_buf;
  tampered[5] = tampered[5] ^ (0xff as u8);
  let dec_tampered: TlsDecryptResult = tls_decrypt_record_rpi4(ref s_hs_key, ref s_hs_iv, 3, ref tampered, recv_len, 22);
  if dec_tampered.ok == 1 { ok = 0; }

  // --- Client verifies CertificateVerify against the (self-test-
  // known) server identity public key -- RFC 7250 pinned-key trust.
  // cert_transcript_hash is still held from the Certificate phase
  // above; msg_buf (currently holding s_fin, no longer needed) is
  // reused to recompute cv_content fresh. ---
  let recomputed_cv_content_len: i64 = tls_certverify_signed_content_rpi4(ref cert_transcript_hash, mut ref msg_buf);
  if ed25519_verify(ref server_id_pub, ref msg_buf, recomputed_cv_content_len, ref cv_sig) != 1 { ok = 0; }

  // --- Client verifies server Finished ---
  let s_verify_data_check: [u8; 32] = tls_finished_verify_data_rpi4(ref s_hs_traffic, ref transcript, t_len_cv);
  if tls_bytes_equal32_rpi4(ref s_verify_data_check, ref s_verify_data) != 1 { ok = 0; }

  // --- Client Finished ---
  let c_verify_data: [u8; 32] = tls_finished_verify_data_rpi4(ref c_hs_traffic, ref transcript, t_len_s_fin);
  let c_fin_len: i64 = tls_build_finished_rpi4(ref c_verify_data, mut ref msg_buf);

  let enc_c_fin: TlsEncryptResult = tls_encrypt_record_rpi4(ref c_hs_key, ref c_hs_iv, 0, ref msg_buf, c_fin_len, 22);
  rec_buf = enc_c_fin.rec;
  recv_len = enc_c_fin.span_len;
  let dec_c_fin: TlsDecryptResult = tls_decrypt_record_rpi4(ref c_hs_key, ref c_hs_iv, 0, ref rec_buf, recv_len, 22);
  if (dec_c_fin.ok != 1) || (dec_c_fin.span_len != c_fin_len) || (tls_bytes_equal512_rpi4(ref dec_c_fin.content, ref msg_buf, c_fin_len) != 1) { ok = 0; }

  let c_verify_data_check: [u8; 32] = tls_finished_verify_data_rpi4(ref c_hs_traffic, ref transcript, t_len_s_fin);
  if tls_bytes_equal32_rpi4(ref c_verify_data_check, ref c_verify_data) != 1 { ok = 0; }

  // --- Application traffic secrets (Master Secret derived from the
  // SAME Handshake Secret, transcript through server Finished only). ---
  let derived_hs: [u8; 32] = tls_derive_secret_rpi4(ref handshake_secret, "derived", ref empty_transcript, 0);
  let master_secret: [u8; 32] = tls_hkdf_extract_rpi4(ref derived_hs, ref zero32);

  let c_ap_traffic: [u8; 32] = tls_derive_secret_rpi4(ref master_secret, "c ap traffic", ref transcript, t_len_s_fin);
  let s_ap_traffic: [u8; 32] = tls_derive_secret_rpi4(ref master_secret, "s ap traffic", ref transcript, t_len_s_fin);

  let c_ap_keys: TlsTrafficKeys = tls_derive_traffic_keys_rpi4(ref c_ap_traffic);
  let c_ap_key: [u8; 32] = c_ap_keys.key;
  let c_ap_iv: [u8; 12] = c_ap_keys.iv;
  let s_ap_keys: TlsTrafficKeys = tls_derive_traffic_keys_rpi4(ref s_ap_traffic);
  let s_ap_key: [u8; 32] = s_ap_keys.key;
  let s_ap_iv: [u8; 12] = s_ap_keys.iv;

  // --- Application data round trip, both directions ---
  let client_msg_str: Str = "dhruva-tls-client-hello-app-data";
  let client_msg_len: i64 = str_len_bytes(client_msg_str);
  ti = 0;
  while ti < client_msg_len { msg_buf[ti] = str_byte_at(client_msg_str, ti) as u8; ti = ti + 1; }

  let enc_c_msg: TlsEncryptResult = tls_encrypt_record_rpi4(ref c_ap_key, ref c_ap_iv, 0, ref msg_buf, client_msg_len, 23);
  rec_buf = enc_c_msg.rec;
  recv_len = enc_c_msg.span_len;
  let dec_c_msg: TlsDecryptResult = tls_decrypt_record_rpi4(ref c_ap_key, ref c_ap_iv, 0, ref rec_buf, recv_len, 23);
  if (dec_c_msg.ok != 1) || (dec_c_msg.span_len != client_msg_len) || (tls_bytes_equal512_rpi4(ref dec_c_msg.content, ref msg_buf, client_msg_len) != 1) { ok = 0; }

  let server_reply_str: Str = "dhruva-tls-server-reply-app-data";
  let server_reply_len: i64 = str_len_bytes(server_reply_str);
  ti = 0;
  while ti < server_reply_len { msg_buf[ti] = str_byte_at(server_reply_str, ti) as u8; ti = ti + 1; }

  let enc_s_reply: TlsEncryptResult = tls_encrypt_record_rpi4(ref s_ap_key, ref s_ap_iv, 0, ref msg_buf, server_reply_len, 23);
  rec_buf = enc_s_reply.rec;
  recv_len = enc_s_reply.span_len;
  let dec_s_reply: TlsDecryptResult = tls_decrypt_record_rpi4(ref s_ap_key, ref s_ap_iv, 0, ref rec_buf, recv_len, 23);
  if (dec_s_reply.ok != 1) || (dec_s_reply.span_len != server_reply_len) || (tls_bytes_equal512_rpi4(ref dec_s_reply.content, ref msg_buf, server_reply_len) != 1) { ok = 0; }

  let _ = uart_puts_rpi4_vani("CRYPTO: TLS 1.3 (TLS_CHACHA20_POLY1305_SHA256, x25519, RFC 7250 raw pubkeys) live handshake + app data round trip");
  if ok == 1 {
    let _ = uart_puts_rpi4_vani(" (PASS)\n");
  } else {
    let _ = uart_puts_rpi4_vani(" (FAIL)\n");
  }
  return ok;
}


// Round 86 -- Phase A step 1 of the networking/SSH/WiFi scope (see
// project memory project_dhruva_networking_ssh_wifi_scope_2026_09_06
// .md): the smallest useful netif abstraction on this port. Loopback/
// synthetic frames only -- deliberately NOT a real GENET or USB/XHCI
// NIC driver, mirroring how even kernel_main.vani's own Pi 1 side
// verifies its netif layer purely via synthetic frames (its real
// LAN9512/CDC-ECM backends have no QEMU device model either, per
// docs/TODO.md's own Tier 3 list) -- confirmed freshly this round
// that QEMU's raspi4b machine has no network device model at all, so
// a real backend has no QEMU verification path regardless of how this
// is designed. The queue itself lives in boot/rpi4/netif_state.S
// (its own header comment has the full ring-buffer design), the same
// "persistent state lives in a small asm accessor file, vani only
// ever holds transient/local state" shape round 84's shell_state.S
// already established on this port.
// 512, not kernel_main.vani's own current 1514-byte real-Ethernet-MTU
// sizing, deliberately -- that Pi 1 side itself started at exactly
// this same 512 ("deliberately smaller than a real Ethernet MTU,
// since nothing this project could test end-to-end under QEMU needed
// a full-size frame", round 66's own comment) and was only raised
// once a real DHCPOFFER actually needed more. Nothing in this round's
// self-test needs more than 16 bytes -- raise this the same way Pi
// 1's netif_frame_slot_size() was raised, once a real later round's
// payload actually needs it, not speculatively now. Also a practical
// vani-language constraint: vani has no array-repeat literal syntax
// (`[0; N]`) and `let` requires a full initializer, so a fixed-size
// local array needs either a fully hand-enumerated literal or a
// helper returning one (netif_zero_frame_rpi4 below) -- 512 elements
// is a far more tractable one-time literal than 1514.
const NETIF_SLOT_SIZE_RPI4: i64 = 512;

extern "C" fn netif_state_reset() -> u32;
extern "C" fn netif_state_count() -> u32;
extern "C" fn netif_state_stage_byte(offset: u32, value: u32) -> u32;
extern "C" fn netif_state_commit_send(frame_len: u32) -> i64;
extern "C" fn netif_state_recv_len() -> i64;
extern "C" fn netif_state_recv_byte(offset: u32) -> u32;
extern "C" fn netif_state_recv_advance() -> u32;

fn netif_get_mtu_rpi4() -> i64 {
  return NETIF_SLOT_SIZE_RPI4 - 14;
}

